---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { legalCompany, legalMailto } from "../lib/legal";
---

<Legal
  title="Cookie Policy — Zenguy"
  description="Cookie policy for Zenguy under article 22.2 LSSI-CE and AEPD guidance."
  heading="Cookie Policy"
  effective="30 August 2026"
>
  <p>
    This policy explains how {legalCompany.legalName} uses cookies and similar
    technologies on {legalCompany.site}, www.zenguy.com and {legalCompany.app}.
    It should be read together with our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What cookies and similar technologies are</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small files stored on your device. Similar technologies include
    local storage and session tokens. Some are set by us (first-party), while
    others are set or read by service providers.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Necessary technologies</h2>
  <p>
    The Zenguy API uses the strictly necessary first-party cookie
    <code>zenguy_rt</code> to renew an authenticated session. It is HttpOnly,
    SameSite=Lax, host-only, limited to <code>/api/auth</code>, Secure in
    production, and expires after at most 30 days. The short-lived access token
    stays only in application memory, not local storage.
  </p>
  <p>
    When Google sign-in starts, the API temporarily sets
    <code>zenguy_google_oauth</code> to validate the OAuth return. It is
    HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, host-only, limited to
    <code>/api/auth/google</code>, Secure in production, and expires after at
    most 10 minutes. Local storage may hold non-authentication UI preferences
    needed to provide the service.
  </p>
  <p>
    On each Zenguy website, local storage under the key
    <code>zenguy:cookie-consent:v1</code> records the preference version, whether
    analytics is allowed, and the date of the choice. We keep that preference
    for up to 12 months on zenguy.com and www.zenguy.com, and for up to 24
    months on app.zenguy.com, so the relevant site can respect it without
    asking on every visit. Browser storage is specific to each origin: a choice
    on zenguy.com, www.zenguy.com or app.zenguy.com is not automatically shared
    with either of the others.
  </p>
  <p>
    If durable local storage cannot save or verify a choice, the same consent
    record can be kept for the current tab in session storage under
    <code>zenguy:cookie-consent-session-override:v1</code>. This fail-safe keeps
    a withdrawal from reverting to an older choice and ends with the browser
    session; it is not sent to Google.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare, which hosts the websites, may set a security cookie, for example
    to distinguish people from bots. It is used to keep the service available
    and safe. Stripe Checkout and the Stripe customer portal, which open only
    when you start or manage a payment, set their own cookies on Stripe’s
    domains to provide that payment service.
  </p>
  <p>
    The public site loads Geist, Geist Mono and Caveat from Google Fonts, and
    the application loads Inter, IBM Plex Mono and Newsreader. These are network requests to Google,
    not Zenguy analytics cookies, and occur independently of the analytics
    choice. Google may receive technical request data such as the IP address
    needed to deliver the font.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Optional Google Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We use Google Analytics 4 to produce aggregated reports about visits and
    how Zenguy is used. On zenguy.com and www.zenguy.com it measures visits and
    use of public pages, validated campaign categories and clicks on links to
    the product. On app.zenguy.com it measures allow-listed, normalized route
    categories, consented signed-in users and a small set of product milestones: completed
    registration, subscription checkout started, and a confirmed paid
    subscription. This helps us improve the public content and product journey.
    We do not use it for advertising, and Google signals and advertising
    personalisation are disabled in our tag configuration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google Analytics remains off on each origin unless you make an affirmative
    choice to allow it there. Before a first acceptance, including after an
    initial rejection, we do not load the Google tag or send Analytics data or
    a consent-status ping. If you withdraw after the tag was loaded, the page
    first sends the denial update needed to revoke Analytics storage and then
    reloads into a tag-free state. We do not deploy this Analytics tag on
    localhost or preview deployments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Application analytics use route templates such as a page category rather
    than workspace or resource URLs. Our analytics events exclude form
    contents, email addresses, tokens, test URLs and workspace or resource
    identifiers. A confirmed subscription event can include the subscription
    product, price and currency, and the Stripe invoice identifier used as a
    transaction identifier; it does not include card details.
  </p>
  <p>
    For signed-in users who consent on app.zenguy.com, the browser derives a
    purpose-specific pseudonymous Analytics User-ID from the opaque internal
    account identifier. The raw identifier, email and name are not sent. The
    derived value supports a more consistent consented-user count across
    browsers and devices. Finite reporting categories can also describe the
    current route section, authentication state, account-age and workspace-count
    band, and current workspace role and subscription status; they never contain
    a workspace identifier or name.
  </p>
  <p>
    If analytics is already allowed when you start a live subscription
    checkout, the application keeps a short-lived session-storage marker for
    that checkout. The marker is ignored after 24 hours and removed when next
    checked or when the browser session ends. It lets the return page match
    the first paid invoice to a checkout initiated in the same browser tab and
    avoid false or duplicate purchase events. The workspace identifier used to
    key that local marker is not sent to Google.
  </p>
  <p>If you allow analytics, the Google Analytics tag may set these first-party cookies:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>_ga</code> — distinguishes users; Google’s default expiry is two
      years.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>_ga_*</code> — persists session state; Google’s default expiry is
      two years.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The provider is <strong>Google Ireland Limited</strong>. Read Google’s
    official information about <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/11397207">GA4 cookie use</a>,
    <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses information from sites that use its services</a>,
    and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google’s Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cookie-preferences">4. Consent and your choices</h2>
  <p>
    The first time you visit each Zenguy origin, its banner offers
    “Accept analytics” and “Reject analytics” at the same level. You can also
    choose through Cookie preferences. Rejecting analytics does not restrict
    access to the site or application.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can revisit your choice at any time using <strong>Cookie settings</strong>
    in the public-site footer, or the persistent <strong>Cookie preferences</strong>
    control in the application. Turning analytics off updates the stored preference
    and removes the Google Analytics cookies visible to that origin. Because
    choices are stored independently, withdraw separately on every Zenguy
    origin where you previously accepted. Withdrawal does not affect processing
    that took place while your earlier consent was in effect. You can also
    delete or block cookies, local storage and session storage in your browser.
    Blocking or deleting the <code>zenguy_rt</code> authentication cookie will
    prevent session renewal and ultimately sign you out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our controls follow a basic Consent Mode v2 implementation. Google’s
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/tag-platform/security/concepts/consent-mode">official Consent Mode description</a>
    explains the difference between basic and advanced implementations. The
    AEPD also publishes a <a href="https://www.aepd.es/guias/guia-cookies.pdf">guide on the use of cookies</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. More information</h2>
  <p>
    The personal data involved in these technologies, their purposes and
    retention are described in the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
    Questions: <a href={legalMailto}>{legalCompany.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
